    "adaptations": "Foundation: shorter distance, slower pace, larger target, and softer equipment.\nIntermediate: standard distance and one decision cue.\nAdvanced: smaller target, weaker side, or later cue without increasing contact.\nLarge group: duplicate simple zones and use performer–observer–recorder trios.\nSmall group: fewer zones and more feedback cycles.\nLimited space: stationary practice and short one-way routes.\nPartial equipment: one item per pair; observer rehearses movement without equipment.\nDifferent needs: adapt position, range, distance, pace, ball size, role, and communication; allow seated or fixed-support participation.",
    "assessment": "Teacher checks: preparation, execution, target adjustment, spacing, and explanation.\nSuccess: four measures are shown consistently and the student explains one cue.\nPartial success: safe execution but a prompt is needed for preparation or decision.\nNot yet successful: spacing or force remains unsafe after adaptation; return to isolated foundation practice.",
    "reflection": "Teacher questions: Who prepared early? Did the organisation create enough practice? Which adaptation improved control?\nStudent questions: What cue helped? What changed after feedback? What will you repeat next time?\nImprovement: adjust one distance, target, group ratio, or cue before BB-05 — off-ball movement and passing lanes.",
    "continuity": "If outcomes are met: proceed to BB-05 — off-ball movement and passing lanes.\nIf not: repeat the foundation action with no decision pressure, one success cue, and a larger target.",
    "bankLink": "Tags: Basketball, school PE, foundation, safety, learning pathway.\nSport: Basketball. Age: 9–11. Equipment: basic.\nSkills: preparation, control, accuracy, decision, and feedback.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: foundation.\nKeywords: set shot, target focus, balanced landing, rebound timing, two-hand secure, side exit, close range, formative assessment.",
    "systemData": "Physical load: moderate\nCognitive load: moderate\nOrganisational load: low–moderate\nStudent independence: moderate",
    "teacherSummary": "3 minutes for goal and safety; 7 for progressive warm-up.\n10 minutes to establish the central technique.\n12 minutes to apply it with one decision.\n8 minutes of inclusive process-scored play.\n5 minutes for feedback, recovery, and collection.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Close-range shooting is where effort becomes a score, and the rebound routine around it is the main safety control under a basket — the most congested area of any court.\nWhat develops? A repeatable release with one leading hand, visual focus on a fixed target, balanced take-off and landing, and the timing discipline to wait outside the shooter's landing space.\nWhy not skip? Students who never build a stable shape shoot two-handed from the chest and keep that pattern for years; and a class that has not rehearsed rebound timing collides under the basket as soon as shots are contested.",
